  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nGiven antecedent rainfall over the past 24 to 48 hours soils \nremain thoroughly saturated across the region. Streams also\nremain high. Additional precipitation rounds this morning, \ntonight, and Thursday night maintain an elevated flash flood \nthreat. Precipitable water values ranging from 1.6 to 1.9 inches\nper model soundings and guidance indicate an airmass capable of\nefficient, high rainfall rates exceeding 1 to 2 inches per \nhour. Flash flood guidance remains significantly suppressed with\nvalues of around inch in 3 hours sufficient for renewed issues.\n\nThe Flood Watch is kept unchanged through Friday morning. \nTrimming of northeastern counties if confidence increases that \nheavier rainfall cores track farther south and west. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nConvective activity over eastern Indiana along an eastward\nadvecting instability gradient may approach western West \nVirginia and northeast Kentucky near or shortly after daybreak. \n\nAdditional convection is anticipated to initiate in warm \nadvection over northeast Indiana and northwest Ohio, tracking \ntoward western counties by late morning. Primary threats with \nthis initial round include localized downbursts and heavy \nrainfall rates. \n\nA second convective complex, originating over the northern High\nPlains, is projected to track southeastward along the upper \nridge into the Ohio Valley this evening and tonight. Given the \nlate arrival time, surface-based instability may already be \nwaning, some what limiting severe wind potential. In saturated \nsoils, even sub-severe wind gusts can uproot trees and cause \npower outages due to lost soil friction. Additional rainfall on \nsaturated basins tonight and Thursday night may renew flash \nflood concerns. \n\nOverall forecast confidence remains low given model spread in \nridge-riding shortwaves and evolution of the broader instability\nfield. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nUpper-level ridging amplifies Friday into Saturday, shifting the\nprimary storm track northward and allowing a brief period for\nsoils to dry out. Temperatures will recover into the mid 80s\nacross the Metro Valley and lowlands with lower precipitation\nprobabilities. By Sunday, the ridge flattens southward,\nreopening the corridor for shortwave impulses to ride along the\nnorthern periphery of the central US. heat dome. Steep mid- \nlevel lapse rates moving out of the Plains will support renewed \nthunderstorm development Sunday into early next week across the\nmiddle of the country, though overall forecast confidence in\nactivity tracking into our forecast area remains low.",
